{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact head 78290c0965a8ebb3762bddcef7cb53ab9a339cd1 for implementation request #9475 (78f0971c), against main 25a7b83785b27ee98f17579e5a8fb238597b31d7. All fourteen effective artifacts were inspected; the prepared binding names reporting artifact #9812 (75c44822) first and the same builder promise #9706. No actionable defect remains in the reviewed change.\n\nReview #9798 (d4a4c681) is answered. publicationTokens.revoke now checks cancellation after every awaited repository lookup, immediately before revokeToken, as well as when an attempt begins after a retry sleep. A timed-out initial lookup sends no provider call; a timed-out retry lookup sends no second call; a retry sleep that outlasts the wait starts no additional lookup. Giving up remains a caller failure, retaining the attempt's or plan 003's cleanup debt and keyed token row for a later bounded pass. The three new production-engine controls verify the debt survives and the later pass revokes by the same ID. My original two independent 25-ms reproductions now pass unchanged against this head, and a third independent retry-sleep control passes too.\n\nArchitecture: the affected Artroom services remain the Git landing driver/storage and canonical mint adapter, plus Room SQLite/alarm coordination. This head implements the adopted R-MINT contract. It changes none of Gitseq's ordinary-Git storage, opaque sequencing kernel, host binding or replaceable Workroom interpreter contracts from docs/reference/architecture.md. Main 25a7b837 is an ancestor. The complete delta from reviewed af89862b touches only engine.ts, landing.test.ts, support.ts and plans/README.md; every other path, including Room, LandingCore, artifacts.ts, the Git harness and Workers tests, mints.ts and docs/protocol.md, is byte-identical. The earlier complete source/test review and current regression gates therefore carry the atomic pushToken/claim transaction, keyed landing rows and one-time fill, point lookups, persisted pre-send wake, Room startup/recovery/nextAlarm wiring, 45-record bounded backlog, unknown retention, and idle/gone-repository fences. The new check preserves the existing revocation API and error behavior.\n\nSecurity: each publication mint remains a 60-second write token with its own durable owner; a non-idempotent retry gets a new record, uncertain creates remain uncertain, and no inventory authorizes attribution or blanket revocation. Known IDs transfer with their owners atomically, and abandoned revocation lookups now stop before the provider send. Mint errors retain safe allow-listed metadata. I checked both engine revocation callers and the ledger's bounded lookup behavior; late-create settlement remains distinct and is preserved. The pre-existing landing provider-text sinks remain open under separate effective request #9796 (d29c09fa), inspected again; this approval does not claim their repair.\n\nThe authority-bearing chain's four facts remain confirmed from the durable record: planner's effective operator foundation #1 and the previously verified role projection at the governing request; explicit contract and lane-edit commissioning request #8651, with implementation in separate requests; independent ratified design approval #9463 and sealed landing #9465 satisfying that commission; and effective unretired, non-stale merged design artifact #9467 before builder's follow-on promise #9706. I re-inspected these events and lane B's exact conditions. Request #9475 requires the adopted design, root gates and separate exact-head review, and prohibits live provider actions and deployment. No new authority or contract adoption is implied.\n\nSimplification: the repair adds one check in the existing bounded path. Retaining the landing owner and a keyed token projection allows the shared ledger to replace duplicated canonical mint/retry behavior without scanning operation bodies. No unused canonicalTokens caller remains. The mints fence reuses the existing fixed coordinator mechanism; pending unknown rows do not create a five-second write loop. No additional simplification change is required.\n\nThe report's eleven carried choices retain their previously reviewed meanings: recovery after a stopped-host fault or double storage failure may need a new object start; rolled-back pushToken releases off the publication queue while retaining debt; landing cleanup rows do not settle by expiry; gone repositories retain records; the last already-stored timeout wake may run once; mints runs last and carries its own bounded failure fence. The ledger's accepted tokens, uncertain creates and each existing owner's cleanup contract remain distinct.\n\nValidation in independent clone /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-b-v2: npm ci, root typecheck, Git Workers (11 passed), Room Wrangler dry-run bundling, and independent Git harness typecheck each exited 0. Root npm test exited 0: 2,251 passed and one skipped, including Git Node 285, Room Node 197 and Room workerd 467. The expected restart and request-failure diagnostics did not fail the framework gates. Both cancellation guards were mutated separately in the isolated guards clone: K1 removing the post-lookup check caused the first and retry lookup tests to fail; K2 removing the attempt-start check caused the retry-sleep test to fail. Both were named runtime failures, with no syntax/load failure or survivor. All temporary source edits were restored. The restored full Git landing file passed all 65 tests, and all three independent checker controls passed. Pristine and guards clones were tracked-clean and diff checks passed. Earlier review #9798's 33 independently runtime-red mutations are historical evidence for the carried guards; this revision freshly tested the two cancellation guards. The canonical checkout remained tracked-clean at main 25a7b837. Transient local open-file launch failures were retried normally and were not counted as gates.\n\nLane C, operator cleanup projections, provider-text hardening, full cost accounting and the complete platform scope remain open. Approval is limited to this exact implementation head; no live credential creation, provider call or deployment was performed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:75c4482203300ac314c915ec3d7af84dd1a8afb7","binding":"assigned","head":"78290c0965a8ebb3762bddcef7cb53ab9a339cd1","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:eb43d87714b09ba7105f467412573bda1b69b88e\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:641edc6594bb238e45f6ee23e4f221a44b8ff921\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:eb43d87714b09ba7105f467412573bda1b69b88e","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}